1. Overview

   The BLADEC module can be used to create a message flow from Kamailio to
   any application that uses blade library.

2. Dependencies

   2.1. Kamailio Modules
   2.2. External Libraries or Applications

2.1. Kamailio Modules

   The following modules must be loaded before this module:
     * tm - (optional) needed only by bladec_async_relay()

2.2. External Libraries or Applications

   The following libraries or applications must be installed before
   running Kamailio with this module loaded:
     * libblade

3. Parameters

   3.1. workers (int)
   3.2. bind_addr (str)
   3.3. event_callback (str)
   3.4. max_clients (int)
   3.5. config (str)

3.1. workers (int)

   Number of worker processes to be started to handle incoming messages
   from remote applications.

   Default value is 1.

   Example 1.1. Set workers parameter
...
modparam("bladec", "workers", 2)
...

3.2. bind_addr (str)

   Local IP and port to listen on for incoming TCP connections.

   Default value is "127.0.0.1:8448".

   Example 1.2. Set bind_addr parameter
...
modparam("bladec", "bind_addr", "1.2.3.4:8228")
...

3.3. event_callback (str)

   The name of the function in the kemi configuration file (embedded
   scripting language such as Lua, Python, ...) to be executed instead of
   event_route[...] blocks.

   The function receives a string parameter with the name of the event,
   the values are: 'bladec:connection-new', 'bladec:connection-closed',
   'bladec:message-received'.

   Default value is 'empty' (no function is executed for events).

   Example 1.3. Set event_callback parameter
...
modparam("bladec", "event_callback", "ksr_bladec_event")
...
-- event callback function implemented in Lua
function ksr_bladec_event(evname)
        KSR.info("===== bladec module triggered event: " .. evname .. "\n");
        return 1;
end
...

3.4. max_clients (int)

   Maximum number of clients that can connect to bladec socket.

   Default value is 8.

   Example 1.4. Set max_clients parameter
...
modparam("bladec", "max_clients", 4)
...

3.5. config (str)

   Path to blade connector config file.

   Default value is "" (not set).

   Example 1.5. Set config parameter
...
modparam("bladec", "config", "/etc/kamailio/kamailio-blade.cfg")
...

4. Functions

   4.1. bladec_relay(evdata)
   4.2. bladec_async_relay(evdata)
   4.3. bladec_multicast(evdata, etag)
   4.4. bladec_async_multicast(evdata, etag)
   4.5. bladec_unicast(evdata, etag)
   4.6. bladec_async_unicast(evdata, etag)
   4.7. bladec_close()
   4.8. bladec_set_tag(tname)

4.1.  bladec_relay(evdata)

   Relay the data given as parameter to connected applications.

   The function is passing the task to bladec dispatcher process,
   therefore the SIP worker process is not blocked. Also, it doesn't wait
   for any response, therefore the processing of the configuration
   continues very fast when executing bladec_relay().

   This function can be used from ANY_ROUTE.

   Example 1.6. bladec_relay usage
...
bladec_relay("{ \"event\": \"test\",\n \"data\": { \"fU\": \"$fU\" }\n}");
...

   The above exaple will send the following message over tcp:

   Example 1.7. TCP message
...
{
 "event": "test",
 "data": { "fU": "test" }
}
...

4.2.  bladec_async_relay(evdata)

   Relay the data given as parameter to connected applications. Before
   evaluating the parameter, the request processing is suspended using tm
   module (using the t_suspend()/t_continue() framework). The routing of
   the SIP request can be continued once
   event_route[bladec:message-received] is triggered. After
   bladec_async_relay() returns true, no relaying should happen in
   request_route(), it should be followed by exit;.

   This function can be used from REQUEST_ROUTE.

   Example 1.8. bladec_async_relay usage
...
bladec_async_relay("{ \"event\": \"suspend\",\n \"data\":"
        " { \"index\": \"$T(id_index)\", \"label\": \"$T(id_label)\" }\n}");
...

4.3.  bladec_multicast(evdata, etag)

   Relay the data given as parameter to connections that match the tag
   provided by etag value. The etag can be a variable. For more see
   bladec_relay() and bladec_set_tag().

   Example 1.9. bladec_multicast usage
...
bladec_multicast("{ \"event\": \"test\",\n \"data\": { \"fU\": \"$fU\" }\n}", "t
agx");
...

4.4.  bladec_async_multicast(evdata, etag)

   Async relay the data given as parameter to connections that match the
   tag provided by etag value. The etag can be a variable. For more see
   bladec_async_relay() and bladec_set_tag().

   Example 1.10. bladec_async_multicast usage
...
bladec_async_multicast("{ \"event\": \"suspend\",\n \"data\":"
    " { \"index\": \"$T(id_index)\", \"label\": \"$T(id_label)\" }\n}", "tagx");
...

4.5.  bladec_unicast(evdata, etag)

   Relay the event data given as parameter to the first connection that
   match the tag provided by etag value. The etag can be a variable. For
   more see bladec_relay() and bladec_set_tag().

   Example 1.11. bladec_unicast usage
...
bladec_unicast("{ \"event\": \"test\",\n \"data\": { \"fU\": \"$fU\" }\n}", "tag
x");
...

4.6.  bladec_async_unicast(evdata, etag)

   Async relay the data given as parameter to the first connection that
   match the tag provided by etag value. The etag can be a variable. For
   more see bladec_async_relay() and bladec_set_tag().

   Example 1.12. bladec_async_unicast usage
...
bladec_async_unicast("{ \"event\": \"suspend\",\n \"data\":"
    " { \"index\": \"$T(id_index)\", \"label\": \"$T(id_label)\" }\n}", "tagx");
...

4.7.  bladec_close()

   Close bladec current client connection.

   This function can be used from ANY_ROUTE.

   Example 1.13. bladec_close usage
...
event_route[bladec:connection-new] {
  if($bladec(srcaddr)!="127.0.0.1") {
    bladec_close();
    exit;
  }
}
...

4.8.  bladec_set_tag(tname)

   Set tag name for current client connection. The parameters has to be a
   string up to 64 characters. It can also be a variable holding such
   string.

   This function can be used from ANY_ROUTE.

   Example 1.14. bladec_set_tag usage
...
event_route[bladec:connection-new] {
  if($bladec(srcaddr)=="127.0.0.1") {
    bladec_set_tag("local");
    exit;
  }
}
...

5. Event Routes

   5.1. bladec:connection-new
   5.2. bladec:connection-closed
   5.3. bladec:message-received

5.1.  bladec:connection-new

   If defined, the module calls event_route[bladec:connection-new] when a
   new client is connected.
...
event_route[bladec:connection-new] {
    xlog("new connection from $bladec(srcaddr):$bladec(srcport)\n");
}
...

5.2.  bladec:connection-closed

   If defined, the module calls event_route[bladec:connection-closed] when
   a client connection is closed.
...
event_route[bladec:connection-closed] {
    xlog("connection closed by $bladec(srcaddr):$bladec(srcport)\n");
}
...

5.3.  bladec:message-received

   If defined, the module calls event_route[bladec:message-received] when
   a message is received from a client.
...
event_route[bladec:message-received] {
    xlog("received [$bladec(msg)] from $bladec(srcaddr):$bladec(srcport)\n");
}
...

6. Exported pseudo-variables

     * $bladec(srcaddr) - source ip
     * $bladec(srcport) - source port
     * $bladec(msg) - received event message
     * $bladec(conidx) - internal connection index

   Exported pseudo-variables are documented at
   https://www.kamailio.org/wiki/.
